# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependencies
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/cache
.yarn/unplugged
.yarn/build-state.yml
.yarn/install-state.gz
.pnpm-store/

# Lockfiles are intentionally tracked. Do not ignore them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.next/
out/
build/
dist/
.vercel/
.turbo/

# Round 6 (B2). Authored fixture SOURCE deliberately placed in directories
# named `dist`, `build` and `coverage`, proving the architecture gate no longer
# treats a directory name as evidence that code is generated. The patterns
# above are name-based and would silently delete these files from the
# repository — the same class of mistake the fixtures exist to catch — so they
# are re-included explicitly. Everything genuinely generated stays ignored.
!tests/fixtures/**/src/dist/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/dist/**
!tests/fixtures/**/src/build/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/build/**
!tests/fixtures/**/src/features/dist/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/features/dist/**
!tests/fixtures/**/src/something/build/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/something/build/**

# Round 7 (B3). This ONE fixture must contain a populated `outDir` on disk: the
# defect being reproduced is an authored source file importing its own project's
# build output, so a checked-out tree with an empty `dist/` reproduces nothing
# and the test would pass for the wrong reason. `--noEmit` is deliberate too —
# the fixture must present a BUILT tree without the suite running a real emit.
#
# Scoped to the exact fixture path rather than to `dist` generally (§18): every
# other `dist/` in the repository, fixtures included, stays ignored.
!tests/fixtures/generated-output-import/apps/app/dist/
!tests/fixtures/generated-output-import/apps/app/dist/**
!tests/fixtures/generated-output-import/apps/app/types/
!tests/fixtures/generated-output-import/apps/app/types/**

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TypeScript / tooling caches
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.tsbuildinfo
# Next writes ONE declaration file, at the application workspace root. The
# pattern is anchored to that path rather than left as a bare filename, for the
# same reason the architecture analyser stopped excluding by basename (Review #4
# B2): `next-env.d.ts` is an ordinary name a repository-local declaration may
# legitimately carry, and a bare pattern silently swallows every one of them.
#
# This is load-bearing, not tidiness. Two fixtures that PROVE the B2 bypass is
# closed are named `next-env.d.ts`; under the bare pattern they were ignored and
# would have vanished on commit, taking the regression test's evidence with them.
/apps/web/next-env.d.ts
.eslintcache
.cache/

